/**
@defgroup lazy read a CBOR message in place, step by step

`cbor::lazy` reads a CBOR message in place. It copies no byte of the
message. Each step reads only the heads up to its child. Include
`<cbor/cbor.hpp>`.

A `cbor::lazy` is a position in one top-level item. Each member function
reads the bytes at the position when it is called. A copy of a lazy is a
second position in the same bytes. Several threads can read one lazy at
the same time.

Each step gives a `std::expected` with a `cbor::error`. A chain of steps
stops at the first error.

@section lazy_make MAKE A LAZY

`cbor::lazy::adopt` and `cbor::lazy::view` make the lazy of the top-level
item. Neither copies the bytes.

- `adopt(std::move(bytes))`: `bytes` is a `std::string`. It moves into
  storage that the lazy owns. An lvalue, a `std::string_view` and a
  literal do not compile.
- `view(owner)`: `owner` is a `std::shared_ptr<std::string const>`. The
  lazy reads `*owner` in place. Every result keeps `owner` alive.
- `view(owner, part)`: `part` is a `std::string_view` into memory that
  `owner`, a `std::shared_ptr<void const>`, keeps alive.

`view` throws `std::logic_error` for a null `owner`. The form with `part`
also throws it for an empty `owner`.

`adopt` and `view` read only the first head. They skip one leading tag 55799.
They do not check that the bytes are one complete item. A step finds a
missing part, and no step reads a byte after the item. The bytes `82 01`
make a lazy, and a read of the second element gives `too_little_data`.

@section lazy_steps STEPS IN A MAP OR AN ARRAY

`at` gives the lazy of one child. `operator[]` gives the same result.

- `at(key)`: the value of the first pair whose key is the text `key`. A
  text key does not match a byte string with the same bytes.
- `at(cbor::key{n})`: the value of the first pair whose key is the
  integer `n`. A bignum key that holds `n` matches too.
- `at(index)`: the element of an array or of a typed array, counted from
  0.

A map with a key twice is no error. `at` gives the first pair.

`operator[]` does not throw for a missing key or a wrong index. The
result is a `std::expected`, so `l["a"]["b"]` does not compile. To read a
chain, use `cbor::lazy::at_path()`.

A step reads the heads from the start of its map or array to the target,
and the head of the target. It reads no byte after the head of the target.
A target that the input does not hold gives `too_little_data` at the
step. A map or an array that claims more items than the bytes left gives
`too_little_data` before the step reads an element.

@section lazy_get READ A VALUE

`get<T>()` reads the value at the lazy as `T`. `get` passes a tag that it
does not interpret.

- an integer type, not `bool`: an integer, or a bignum (tag 2 or 3) that
  fits `T`.
- `double`: a float of 16, 32 or 64 bits, or an integer or a bignum that
  a `double` holds exactly.
- `bool`: `false` or `true`.
- `std::nullptr_t`: `null`.
- `cbor::simple_value`: a simple value, for example `undefined`.
- `std::string_view`: a text string, as a `cbor::ref<std::string_view>`.
- `std::span<std::byte const>`: a byte string, as a
  `cbor::ref<std::span<std::byte const>>`.
- `cbor::typed_array`: a typed array (RFC 8746), as a
  `cbor::ref<cbor::typed_array>`.
- `std::float128_t`, where the compiler has it: an element of a typed
  array of 128-bit floats.

A `cbor::ref` views the bytes and keeps their owner alive. Nothing is
copied.

The binary16 float `1.5` (`f9 3e 00`) reads as `1.5`. The integer `42`
reads as `42.0` for `double`. The bignum `2(h'05')` reads as `5` for
`std::int64_t`.

@section lazy_loop THE COUNT, THE ELEMENTS AND THE PAIRS

`size()` gives the count of the head of an array, a map or a typed
array. It reads only the head. It gives a `std::expected` of
`std::uint64_t`. `empty()` gives `true` when the count is 0.

`elements()` gives the elements of an array, or of a typed array, as a
view. Each element is a `std::expected<cbor::lazy, cbor::error>`. The view
reads one element at each step. It is a forward range, a sized range and
a borrowed range, so the standard algorithms take it. It has no `front()`.
Its `size()` gives the count of the head, and it gives exactly that many
values.

`entries()` gives the pairs of a map, in the order of the bytes, as a
view. Each element is a `std::expected` of a pair of lazy values, or of a
`cbor::error`. The key is `first` and the value is `second`.
A key that occurs twice occurs twice in the view. The view is a forward
range, a sized range and a borrowed range.

An iterator holds the owner of the bytes. It stays valid after the lazy
and the view end. When the iterator cannot step over an element, `*it`
gives `too_little_data` or another error of the bytes. Each later position
gives that error.

@section lazy_clone COPY ONE ITEM TO A TREE

`clone()` gives a `std::expected<cbor::owning<cbor::item>, cbor::error>`.
It reads the whole item at the lazy, checks every head, and builds one
`cbor::item` for each data item. The tree copies each text and byte
string. It does not view the bytes of the lazy, and it stays valid after
the lazy and its owner end. The errors of `cbor::decode` apply.

`clone` keeps tags 28 and 29 as tag items. A tag 55799 inside the
top-level item stays a tag item.

`type()` gives a `std::expected<cbor::type, cbor::error>`, the kind of the
value at the lazy.

- A tag 2 or 3 whose magnitude fits 64 bits gives `unsigned_integer` or
  `negative_integer` (RFC 8949, section 3.4.3).
- A larger bignum gives `unsigned_bignum` or `negative_bignum`.
- A typed array (RFC 8746) gives `typed_array`.
- Every other tag gives `tag`. `type()` does not pass the tag.

@section lazy_errors ERRORS

Each step gives a `cbor::error`:

- `key_not_found`: no pair has the key.
- `index_out_of_bounds`: the index is not less than the count.
- `not_indexable`: a key or an index on a value of the wrong kind; `size()`
  on a value that is not an array, a map or a typed array; `elements()`
  on a value that is not an array; `entries()` on a value that is not a
  map.
- `incorrect_type`: `get<T>()` of another kind of value.
- `number_out_of_range`: a number that does not fit `T`.
- `too_little_data`: the bytes end before the item, or a count or a string
  is longer than the bytes left.
- `syntax_error`: a head is broken.
- `indefinite_length`: `elements()` or `entries()` of an array or a map of
  indefinite length, or `clone()` of such an item.
- `nesting_depth_exceeded`: `clone()` of an item that nests too deep.
- `not_enough_memory`: an allocation of the owner in `adopt` fails.

A wrong use throws `std::logic_error`. It throws for a null `owner` in
`view`, and for an empty `owner` in the form with `part`. It throws for
each member function of a lazy that holds no top-level item, for example
a `cbor::lazy{}`.

@manonly
.SH "EXAMPLES"
@endmanonly
@snippet doc_reference_lazy.cpp lazy group
*/
